      "message": "Could we propagate `NO_SLOTS` without freeing the packet when the chained payload exceeds the currently available WQEBBs? `rdma_mlx5_tso_append_chain()` returns zero for both the hardware DS limit and transient slot exhaustion, and this branch consumes/frees both. For example, 254 SENDs on an empty 256-slot queue leave two slots: a GSO packet with a 54-byte header and four 2K buffers passes the initial two-slot check but needs three WQEBBs, so it is immediately dropped instead of retrying after the SEND prefix completes. The eMPW fallback has the same behavior. A source-derived ASAN/UBSan harness reproduces this; the same packet posts successfully with three slots. Please distinguish the transient case, as `rdma_mlx5_build_send()` already does.",
